  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 104 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nAs the aforementioned deep upper trough emerges onto the South\nPlains into Monday, a sfc low will develop over eastern CO and \nstrengthen to near 990-mb as it moves onto the north-central \nPlains on Monday. \n\n* STORM CHANCES *\n\nStorm chances return to the forecast area Sunday night, expected\nto form initially along a dry line over eastern NM and sweep east\nduring the overnight hours. Instability will be limited as mid-\nlevel and low-level lapse rates appear quite weak, but a few \nhundred J/kg of MUCAPE should still be present. Despite the weak \ninstability, dynamic wind fields will result in strong shear \nsupporting at least some severe potential Sun night (850mb winds \napproaching 50 kts). Downdrafts transporting strong winds to the \nsfc will be the main severe hazard, but there is non-zero tornado \npotential. Some supporting factors for the tornado potential \ninclude moist low- levels and the strong low-level wind shear (30+\nkts in the lowest kilometer), but the diurnal timing is \nunfavorable, a small inversion lingers near 850mb, and the 0-3 km \nshear vectors are nearly parallel to the boundary (all of which \nlower the tornado potential). The storm chances Sunday night are \nmainly focused across the southwest TX Panhandle and the northern \nSouth Plains. \n\nThat dryline quickly progresses east on Monday. Much of the area\nwill be capped to sfc-based convection through early afternoon, \nbut a few storms could fire over the eastern Rolling Plains during\nthe afternoon. A conditional severe threat exists Monday \nafternoon, dependent upon the storms actually forming in our area.\nHowever, most guidance favors storms forming just to the east of \nour forecast area, and the NBM had just 10% PoPs over our eastern \ncounties Mon PM. \n\n* BREEZY WINDS MONDAY *\n\nFollowing the dryline passage, boundary layer mixing deepens\nconsiderably to as high as 700mb on Monday, allowing strong low- \nlevel winds to mix to the sfc. ECMWF Ens members are coming into \nbetter agreement on the max gusts, with most members showing 35-40\nmph at Lubbock, and slightly higher as you go north towards \nPlainview (40- 45 mph). Forecast soundings suggest these values \nmay even be a tad underdone, showing wind speeds of 45-55 mph \nwithin the 700-800mb layer (upper portion of the PBL). With that \nin mind, used the NBM 90th percentile to boost the wind gust \nforecast higher than the original model blend. The breezy winds \npersist on Tuesday, but aren't expected to be as strong as Monday.\n\nWinds on Monday will generally be out of the west-southwest, but a\ncold frontal passage Mon night will swing the winds to north- \nnorthwesterly for Mon night and Tuesday. This post-frontal airmass\nwill offer some cooler nights, with lows in the low 40s on the \nCaprock each night Tues-Thurs, but a freeze appears unlikely over \nthe next week (less than a 10% chance per the NBM). The typical \nfirst freeze is right around November 1st. The upper pattern \nremains progressive, so we'll be keeping an eye on additional \ndisturbances bringing more rain chances late week into next \nweekend.",
